<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The K16 as a RESIDENT murakumo node.

  Until 2026-09-08 the rig was `nohup python3 ... &` typed by hand, with its
  artifact and its state in /tmp. That is a node for as long as somebody is
  watching: nothing restarted it, and a reboot of this Mac took the served
  BOOTX64.EFI with it, so the board would have PXE-booted into nothing.

  WHAT IS AND IS NOT IN HERE. Paths only. A LaunchAgent plist is world-readable,
  so the node's private key is NOT here -- it is at ~/.gftd/k16-murakumo-node.pem
  (mode 600) and this file names the path. kagi cannot be used from launchd at
  all (it has no way to raise a Keychain unlock prompt), which is why the key has
  a ~/.gftd copy in the first place; see the secrets-location-map skill.

  The program is the WEST CHECKOUT, not a worktree. ~/.claude/worktrees entries
  are removed by scripts/worktree-retire.cljs once they are landed, clean and
  idle, so a resident service pointed at one is a service that stops working on
  a schedule nobody associates with it.

  Paths are absolute because launchd does not expand $HOME in
  EnvironmentVariables. There is one K16, so they are written out rather than
  templated.

  ⚠ RESTARTING THIS SERVICE CAN COST A POWER CYCLE. The board PXE-fetches the
  image on every one of its ~40-second boots, so a restart has a real chance of
  landing inside a transfer. Measured 2026-09-08, the first time this service
  was installed: the server was replaced while the firmware was reading, the
  board took whatever it got, and it has been silent ever since -- no DHCP, no
  ARP, zero packets on either NIC. The kernel's fuel guard is `ud2` with no
  handler and nothing resets it (ADR-0203), so recovery is a human at the power
  button, which is the one thing this rig exists not to need.

  Restart between boots, not during one: watch for the run-end byte on the
  netlog (DE) or for a hello on the bus3 sink, and act in the ~39 seconds the
  board spends rebooting. `launchctl kickstart -k` is immediate and does not
  wait for anything.

  Install:
    cp os/aiueos/deploy/com.gftd.k16-murakumo-node.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.gftd.k16-murakumo-node.plist
  Stop:
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.gftd.k16-murakumo-node
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.gftd.k16-murakumo-node</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
    <string>python3</string>
    <string>/Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki/orgs/kotoba-lang/aiueos/os/aiueos/tools/k16-pxe-server.py</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <!-- A server, not an interval job: restart it whenever it stops. The throttle
       keeps a missing interface from becoming a hot loop -- the bus3 thread
       refuses BY NAME when it cannot bind, and that refusal should be readable
       in the log rather than scrolled away. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key><true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key><integer>30</integer>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>

    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_INTERFACE</key><string>en15</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_BOOT</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/BOOTX64.EFI</string>
    <!-- Both NICs of the one board: the firmware netboots from whichever has
         link first, and accepting only one is how the board got stuck. -->
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_EXPECTED_MAC</key><string>70:70:fc:0b:b6:32,70:70:fc:0b:b6:31</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_NETLOG_PORT</key><string>7797</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_CONTROL_PORT</key><string>7798</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_CONTROL_STATE</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/control-nonce</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_NEXT_BOOT_STATE</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/next-boot</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_SERVE_DIR</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16</string>

    <!-- LAN2/bus3: the murakumo relay's wire. The sink receipts land beside the
         server log so one directory holds the whole rig's record. -->
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_BUS3_RELAY</key><string>1</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_BUS3_INTERFACE</key><string>en8</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_PXE_BUS3_SINK</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/bus3-en8.log</string>

    <!-- The node signs for itself; no shared operator secret is involved. -->
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_NODE_NAME</key><string>gmktec-k16-lan2</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_NODE_DID_FILE</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16-murakumo-node.did</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_NODE_KEY_FILE</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16-murakumo-node.pem</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_CACAO_MINT</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki/orgs/kotoba-lang/aiueos/os/aiueos/tools/k16-cacao-mint.cljk</string>
    <!-- The mint's classpath. `text/src` joined it on 2026-09-09 because
       k16-cacao-mint.cljs moved from clojure.string to kotoba.lang.text
       (aiueos e4187d5, 07:19) and this file did not follow. The resident node
       had been failing every mint since:

         AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_CACAO_REFUSED ... Could not find namespace: kotoba.lang.text

       and the symptom downstream was `stage=enroll status=401`, which reads as
       a signing problem and is not one. A source change landed without its
       deployment counterpart; nothing checks that these two agree. -->
  <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_CACAO_CLASSPATH</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki/orgs/kotoba-lang/org-chainagnostic-cacao/src:/Users/junkawasaki/github/com-junkawasaki/orgs/kotoba-lang/text/src</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_TRUST_TIER</key><string>community</string>
    <key>AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_JOB_QUALIFICATION</key><string>1</string>
  </dict>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/server.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/k16/server.log</string>
  <!-- Adaptive, not Background.  `Background` is not merely a CPU priority on
       macOS: it also applies IOPOL_THROTTLE to the process's disk I/O.  This
       daemon answers a TFTP OACK handshake whose whole budget is one second
       per retry, six retries, and the board's firmware gives up after about
       five minutes of failures.
       Measured 2026-09-08: with the workstation at load average 126 from
       concurrent sessions, a restart of this job took over ten minutes to
       reach its first `print`.  A `sample` of the stopped process showed every
       stack in `os_listdir` inside Python's own codec-registry import -- an
       ordinary stdlib directory scan, throttled.  During that window nothing
       was bound to UDP 67 or 69, so the board could DHCP and never fetch.
       Adaptive starts at background priority and is promoted to standard when
       the process is doing work, which is the behaviour this wants: cheap when
       the board is quiet, responsive the moment it PXE-boots. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Adaptive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
